## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced Terraform design across multi-cloud per-tenant isolation, secret management, and policy enforcement. It also probes testing discipline and scalable module composition.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliases per tenant
- Tenant-scoped remote backends with encryption
- Vault-backed credentials retrieval
- Per-tenant RBAC and network segmentation
- Idempotence testing with Terratest
- Policy gates with OPA or Sentinel

## Code Example

```hcl
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({ cloud = string, region = string }))
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "tenant_${each.key}"
  region = var.tenants[each.key].region
  # fetch creds from Vault here
}

provider "azurerm" {
  alias   = "tenant_${each.key}"
  features = {}
}

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    key = "state/${each.key}/terraform.tfstate"
    encryption = true
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the design to support adding a new cloud to the tenant mix?
- How would you structure tests to run in parallel for many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of multi-account Terraform setups with minimal code duplication, demonstrating proficiency with provider aliases, wrapper modules, and CI gates to enforce environment boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases for multi-account AWS access
- Backend/workspace separation for state isolation
- Wrapper modules for environment context management
- CI gates to enforce production change protections

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "${terraform.workspace}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sandbox"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

module "environment" {
  source = "./modules/environment-wrapper"
  providers = {
    aws = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? aws.prod : aws.sandbox
  }
  environment = terraform.workspace
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design cross-cloud disaster recovery with strict tenant isolation, governance enforcement, and automated validation—a realistic, high-stakes enterprise scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud provider aliasing and per-tenant state backends
- DR readiness flags and retention policies as code
- Shared module registry usage for multi-tenant DR fabric
- Automated testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example
```terraform
# Minimal skeleton showing per-tenant aliasing
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "primary"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "dr"
  project = var.dr_project
}

# Per-tenant state backend
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tenant-${var.tenant_id}-state"
    key    = "primary/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

# DR readiness flag
variable "dr_enabled" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

# DR submodule mirrors critical resources to GCP when enabled
module "dr_replication" {
  source  = "../modules/dr-replication"
  count   = var.dr_enabled ? 1 : 0
  providers = {
    aws.primary = aws.primary
    google.dr   = google.dr
  }
}
```

## Testing Strategy
```go
func TestPrimaryCreatesResources(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/primary",
    }
    defer terraform.Destroy(t, terraformOptions)
    terraform.InitAndApply(t, terraformOptions)

    assertResourceCount(t, terraform.OutputRequired(t, terraformOptions, "resource_ids"), 1)
}

func TestDRNoOpWhenDisabled(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/dr",
        Vars:          map[string]interface{}{"dr_enabled": false},
    }
    plan := terraform.Plan(t, terraformOptions)
    assertEmptyPlan(t, plan)
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design scalable, isolated multi-tenancy infrastructure using Terraform across multiple AWS accounts while leveraging a shared module registry, enforcing policy gates, and validating changes with automated tests.

## Key Concepts

- **Terraform provider aliases and multi-backend strategy**: Enabling isolated state management per tenant
- **Per-tenant resource isolation**: Dedicated VPCs, subnets, and security groups preventing cross-tenant access
- **Central module registry**: Single source of truth for tenant infrastructure configurations
- **CMK-based encryption for Redis**: Ensuring tenant data at rest is encrypted with customer-managed keys
- **CI policy gates for module updates**: Preventing production changes when shared dependencies are modified
- **Idempotence testing with Terratest**: Validating that Terraform configurations produce consistent, repeatable results

## Code Example

```hcl
# tenant-module/main.tf
variable "tenant_id" {}
variable "aws_account_id" {}

provider "aws" {
  alias = "tenant"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::${var.aws_account_id}:role/terraform-access"
  }
}

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    # Per-tenant bucket/key configured at runtime
  }
}

module "vpc" {
  source  = "app.terraform.io/org/vpc/aws"
  version = "2.0.0"

  providers = {
    aws = aws.tenant
  }

  tenant_id = var.tenant_id
  environment = "prod"
}

module "redis" {
  source  = "app.terraform.io/org/redis/aws"
  version = "1.5.0"

  providers = {
    aws = aws.tenant
  }

  tenant_id         = var.tenant_id
  cmk_key_arn       = var.cmk_arn
}
```

## Terratest Example

```go
func TestTenantRedisIdempotence(t *testing.T) {
  for _, tenant := range tenants {
    terraform.Init(t, &terraform.Options{TenantID: tenant})
    terraform.Apply(t, &terraform.Options{TenantID: tenant})

    plan := terraform.Plan(t, &terraform.Options{
      TenantID:  tenant,
      PlanOnly:  true,
    })

    require.Empty(t, plan.Changes, "Expected zero changes for %s", tenant)
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your expertise in multi-account AWS infrastructure management using Terraform, testing your ability to handle complex scenarios involving provider configuration, cross-account networking, state management, policy enforcement, and automated testing.

## Key Concepts
- **Provider aliases**: Enable managing multiple AWS accounts within the same Terraform configuration
- **assume_role**: Secure cross-account access using IAM role assumption
- **remote_state**: Access outputs from other Terraform state files
- **Cross-account VPC peering**: Establish network connectivity between VPCs in different AWS accounts
- **Policy as code**: Implement governance using Sentinel or OPA policies
- **CI testing**: Validate infrastructure changes using Terratest

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "shared"
  region = "us-east-1"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/TerraformRole"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "shared_vpc" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "shared-infrastructure-state"
    key    = "vpc/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection" "prod_to_shared" {
  provider        = aws.prod
  vpc_id        = aws_vpc.prod.id
  peer_vpc_id   = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.vpc_id
  peer_account_id = data.terraform_remote_state.shared_vpc.outputs.account_id
  auto_accept   = false
  tags = {
    Name        = "prod-to-shared-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
    Environment = "production"
  }
}

resource "aws_vpc_peering_connection_accepter" "shared_accepter" {
  provider                  = aws.shared
  vpc_peering_connection_id = aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.id
  auto_accept               = true
  tags = {
    Name        = "shared-accept-prod-peering"
    CostCenter  = "engineering"
  }
}

# Sentinel policy example
policy "vpc_peering_policy" {
  description = "Enforce DNS resolution and CostCenter tagging for VPC peering"

  enforce {
    condition = all {
      aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.enable_dns_support
      contains(aws_vpc_peering_connection.prod_to_shared.tags["CostCenter"], "engineering")
    }
  }
}
```

## CI Test (Terratest)
```go
tfunc TestVPCCrossAccountPeering(t *testing.T) {
  terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
    TerraformDir: "./",
    Vars: map[string]interface{}{
      "environment": "test",
    },
  }

  planOutput := terraform.InitAndPlan(t, terraformOptions)

  assert.Contains(t, planOutput, "aws_vpc_peering_connection")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group")
  assert.NotContains(t, planOutput, "aws_security_group_rule")

  planJSON := terraform.PlanJson(t, terraformOptions)

  peeringResources := planJSON.Configuration.RootModule.Resources
  assert.Equal(t, 1, len(peeringResources))

  for _, resource := range peeringResources {
    assert.Equal(t, "aws_vpc_peering_connection", resource.Type)
  }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates enterprise-grade Terraform practices with proper separation of concerns, governance, and automated validation.
